---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import ProvidersDirectory from '../../components/ProvidersDirectory.astro';
import { resolvePath } from '../../utils/paths.js';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Supported Providers"
  description="Capture jobs from 100+ ATS platforms and job boards inside your own logged-in browser session — no server-side scraping, no proxies."
  currentPath={resolvePath('/docs/providers/')}
  category="Ecosystem"
>
  <h1>Where JobFoundry Captures Jobs</h1>
  <p>
    The extension's first job is to <strong>read the posting you are already looking at, in the browser you are already logged into</strong>. Capture is a request from your own session — not from a datacenter IP and not through a scraping proxy. That is why a board that blocks anonymous crawlers still works when you are signed in.
  </p>
  <p>
    The extension ships with <strong>100+ adapters</strong>: the ATS platforms most employers actually run on, the large aggregators, and a long tail of regional and remote-first boards. Separately, the bundled employer registry indexes <strong>43,000+ company career pages</strong>, so a scan can target one specific employer instead of a whole board.
  </p>

  <div class="doc-callout">
    <div class="callout-title">
      <span>◈</span>
      <span>A sample, not the full list</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      The table below is a representative sample of well-known platforms. The full registry is much larger and changes with every sync — run <code>npm run gen:provider-index</code> in the extension workspace to see every adapter that ships. Don't see the board you use? <a href={resolvePath('/feedback/')}>Ask for it</a> and include a sample posting URL.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sample Providers</h2>
  <p>
    Filter by category, or search for a platform by name:
  </p>

  <ProvidersDirectory />

  <h2>How Capture Works</h2>
  <p>
    Three layers, all running inside the browser:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Background adapters</strong> — one module per platform in
      <code>extension/src/background/vendored/providers/</code>. Each exports a
      standard <code>detect()</code> / <code>fetch()</code> contract and reads
      public ATS JSON endpoints, JSON-LD, feeds, or markup. Zero LLM calls.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Session extractors</strong> — for boards that only render results
      to a logged-in user (LinkedIn, Indeed, Glassdoor, Naukri), readers live in
      <code>extension/src/content/extractors/</code> and run in the page you
      already have open.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Generic fallback</strong> — if an unrecognized site publishes
      standard <code>schema.org/JobPosting</code> JSON-LD, it still gets captured.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>{`// Provider contract (background)
export default {
  id: 'greenhouse',       // unique across all providers
  detect(entry) { ... },  // claim a scan target → { url } or null
  async fetch(entry, ctx) { ... }, // return normalized Job[]
};`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    When a scan runs, the extension resolves each configured target to a provider
    (an explicit <code>provider:</code> id wins, otherwise the first matching
    <code>detect()</code>), fetches listings inside your session, then runs
    liveness checks, normalization, and dedup before shipping the survivors to
    your local ingest API. The server never reaches out to a job board — when it
    needs page content it enqueues a task the extension leases.
  </p>

  <h2>Contributing a New Extractor</h2>
  <p>
    Adding support for a new job board is usually a small, self-contained module. Read our <a href="https://github.com/Covai-Labs/JobFoundry/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Contributing Guide</a> to submit a pull request.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
